==================================================================
DAY 10 - PART B: LAB
Labels, selectors, and a self-healing ReplicaSet
Time: 75-90 minutes
==================================================================

cd ~/k8s-journey/manifests/week2


STEP 1: Play with labels
------------------------
k run a --image=nginx:1.27 --labels=app=web,env=dev
k run b --image=nginx:1.27 --labels=app=web,env=prod
k run c --image=nginx:1.27 --labels=app=api,env=dev

k get pods --show-labels
k get pods -l app=web
k get pods -l env!=prod
k get pods -l 'env in (dev,test)'
k get pods -L app,env

Add, change and remove labels:
  k label pod c tier=backend
  k label pod c env=prod --overwrite
  k label pod c tier-
  k get pods --show-labels

Add an annotation:
  k annotate pod a owner="naveen"
  k describe pod a | grep -A2 Annotations

Clean up:
  k delete pod a b c


STEP 2: Create a ReplicaSet
---------------------------
vi rs-web.yaml

apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: ReplicaSet
metadata:
  name: web-rs
spec:
  replicas: 3
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: web
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: web
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: web
        image: nginx:1.27

k apply -f rs-web.yaml
k get rs
k get pods -o wide --show-labels

Note the pod names: web-rs-<random>. Note which nodes they landed on.


STEP 3: Self-healing
--------------------
Terminal 2: k get pods -w

Terminal 1:
  POD=$(k get pods -l app=web -o name | head -1)
  k delete $POD

A new pod appears within seconds. Delete two at once and watch again.

Look at the owner:
  k get $(k get pods -l app=web -o name | head -1) -o jsonpath='{.metadata.ownerReferences[0].kind}/{.metadata.ownerReferences[0].name}{"\n"}'


STEP 4: Scale
-------------
k scale rs web-rs --replicas=5
k get pods
k scale rs web-rs --replicas=2
k get pods

Change replicas in the YAML to 4 and "k apply -f rs-web.yaml".
Which approach would a team prefer, and why? (Hint: Git.)


STEP 5: Orphan a pod by changing its label
------------------------------------------
POD=$(k get pods -l app=web -o name | head -1)
k label $POD app=debug --overwrite
k get pods --show-labels

You now have 4 "app=web" pods AND one "app=debug" pod. The ReplicaSet
let go of the relabelled pod and created a replacement. This trick is
used in real life to pull a misbehaving pod out for debugging.

k delete pod -l app=debug


STEP 6: Adoption of a matching bare pod
---------------------------------------
k run intruder --image=busybox:1.36 --labels=app=web -- sleep 3600
k get pods --show-labels

The ReplicaSet counts "intruder" as one of its pods, so it now has one
too many and deletes a pod to get back to 4. Lesson: keep labels
unique per app.


STEP 7: Template change does NOT update pods
--------------------------------------------
In rs-web.yaml change the image to nginx:1.26 and apply.
  k get pods -o jsonpath='{range .items[*]}{.metadata.name}{"  "}{.spec.containers[0].image}{"\n"}{end}'
All still show 1.27. Delete one pod; the new one gets 1.26.
This manual process is what Deployments automate.


STEP 8: Delete
--------------
k delete rs web-rs            # deletes pods too
k get pods

Optional: delete only the RS but keep pods:
  k apply -f rs-web.yaml
  k delete rs web-rs --cascade=orphan
  k get pods                  # pods remain, now unmanaged
  k delete pod -l app=web


STEP 9: Commit
--------------
cd ~/k8s-journey && git add . && git commit -m "day10: replicasets and labels"
